.TH CUT2 1 "September 2026" "cut2 2.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
cut2 \- quote-aware field/character selection with regex delimiters

.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cut2
(\-f LIST | \-c LIST) [\-d DELIM] [FILE...]

.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.B cut2
command extracts fields or characters from input files or standard input. Unlike GNU cut, its field delimiter
(\-d) is a full Python regular expression rather than a single literal character, and it treats single- or
double-quoted spans as one field even if they contain the delimiter pattern.

By default, fields are separated by one or more tabs or spaces ([\et ]+).

.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-f LIST
Select fields to extract. LIST is a comma-separated list of field numbers and ranges (e.g., 1,3,5-7). Fields
are delimited by the pattern given with \-d, or by default [\et ]+ (one or more tabs/spaces). Mutually
exclusive with \-c.

.TP
.B \-c LIST
Select specific character positions. LIST is a comma-separated list of positions and ranges (e.g., 1-3,5,7-).
Mutually exclusive with \-f.

.TP
.B \-d DELIM
Use DELIM as the field delimiter regular expression. Default is [\et ]+ (one or more tabs or spaces). Only
meaningful with \-f.

.SH FIELD LISTS
Field and character lists support:
.TP
.B N
The Nth field or character (starting from 1).
.TP
.B N\-M
Fields or characters from N to M, inclusive.
.TP
.B N\-
Fields or characters from N to the end of the line.
.TP
.B N,M,P
Any combination of the above, comma separated. A field or character may be selected more than once, and
selections are printed in the order given (not sorted).

.SH QUOTING
While splitting fields, a run of text opened with a single or double quote is treated as a single field
even if it contains the delimiter pattern. The quote characters themselves are
.B preserved
in the output field (they are not stripped). A backslash immediately before the matching quote character
prevents that quote from closing the span. Quoting has no effect in character mode (\-c).

.SH EXAMPLES
Extract the second field from space/tab-separated input:
.RS
.B echo 'John    Doe    30' | cut2 \-f2
.br
Doe
.RE

Extract a field containing embedded spaces via quoting (quotes are kept):
.RS
.B echo 'John "Doe Smith" 30' | cut2 \-f2
.br
"Doe Smith"
.RE

Extract fields 1 and 3 from comma-separated values:
.RS
.B echo 'Alice,Bob,25' | cut2 \-f1,3 \-d','
.br
Alice\et25
.RE

Extract fields using a regex delimiter (comma or semicolon):
.RS
.B echo 'a,b;c,d' | cut2 \-f2,4 \-d'[,;]'
.br
b\etd
.RE

Extract character positions 1-3:
.RS
.B echo "abcdef" | cut2 \-c1\-3
.br
abc
.RE

.SH NOTES
.IP \(bu 2
The output field delimiter is always a single tab, regardless of the input delimiter pattern.
.IP \(bu 2
An invalid \-d regex causes the program to exit with an error before reading any input.
.IP \(bu 2
Lines are decoded as UTF\-8; "characters" in \-c mode are Unicode code points, not bytes.

.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success
.TP
.B 1
A named input file could not be opened, or the \-d pattern is not a valid regex.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error (missing or conflicting options).

.SH AUTHOR
Chris Pollitt.

.SH SEE ALSO
.BR cut (1),
.BR awk (1),
.BR re (7)
